The charring of sugar is its dehydration process. When sugar is treated with conc. $\mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{SO}_4$, water molecules gets escaped and sugar charcoal is formed. Due to its further reaction with acid, smell of $\mathrm{SO}_2$ is obtained.

\begin{array}{rl}
\mathrm{C}_{12} \mathrm{H}_{22} \mathrm{O}_{11}+\left[\mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{SO}_4\right] & \longrightarrow \\
\text { Sugar } & 12 \mathrm{C}+\left[\mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O}+\mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{SO}_4\right] \\
& \text { Sugar charcoal } \\
\mathrm{C}+2 \mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{SO}_4 & 2 \mathrm{SO}_2+\mathrm{CO}_2+2 \mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O}
\end{array}
